//
// The distance field — how far every hex is from the core, by a
// route an enemy of one class can actually walk.
//
// Plan 11 phase F2.  It answers ONE question — "how many steps from
// here to the core?" — and F3 reads the arrows out of it, F5 moves
// enemies down it, F7 runs a second one with the walls passable to
// decide where a blocked enemy goes.
//
// ── Built from the NEIGHBOUR RELATION, never from coordinates ────
//
// A distance field is a graph property: correct in ANY lattice, as
// long as it is built from the world's own neighbour function.  This
// file therefore calls `lat_neighbour` and never touches `q` / `r`
// arithmetically — see `lattice.loft`, which is the only place in
// dryopea allowed to step a coordinate.
//
// That is what kept this independent of plan 09's conversion to
// pointy-top odd-r: the lattice moved underneath and not one distance
// here changed.  Its gate fell out for free — **the expectations in
// `tests/11_f2_flow_field.loft` survived plan 09 unchanged**, and so
// did all 233 of `scripts/validate.sh`'s measurements.  If a single
// expected distance ever moves under a lattice change, this rule was
// broken somewhere, and what broke is moros#10 all over again.
//
// ── Three states, and why UNREACHABLE is a big number ───────────
//
// dryopea stores only painted hexes, so a field over it has three
// states and not two: *at the core* (0), *n steps away*, and *no
// route at all*.  The natural loft default for an absent entry is 0
// — which is the first of them.  Conflating them makes a walled-off
// spawn read as **arrived**, the same class of defect as
// `EditorInput`'s `-1`-becomes-`0` sentinel (plan 08 § Neutral must
// be the zero value).
//
// So no-route is an explicit value.  It is a LARGE one, not `-1`, and
// that choice is load-bearing: F3 validates "every arrow reaches the
// core in exactly `distance` steps" and F5 moves an enemy to "the
// best free neighbour", both of which mean *the neighbour with the
// SMALLEST distance*.  Against a `-1`, a cell with no route is the
// most attractive neighbour on the map — so the mover would walk
// enemies into exactly the places they cannot go.  Made larger than
// any real distance, the ordering does the right thing on its own
// and no caller has to remember a special case.
//
// ── What the field is over: SURFACE nodes, STEP edges ───────────
//
// Plan 11 phase F6.  A cell joins the field when the class can stand
// on its SURFACE; an edge is walkable when the class can take that
// STEP.  Those are two different questions and the split is
// load-bearing:
//
// **Filtering cells by `can_occupy` instead would make the height
// rule vacuous.**  `can_occupy(x)` means `height(x)` is within a climb
// of its LOWEST standable neighbour, so any two adjacent occupiable
// hexes have a legal step between them in both directions by
// construction — the edge check could be deleted and nothing would
// notice.  Measured while building F6, and it is why the node filter
// is the surface alone (`passable.loft` § What a POSITION can still
// honestly say).
//
// ⚠ **The BFS runs OUTWARD and the enemy walks INWARD, so the step is
// checked backwards.**  Expanding a labelled cell `a` to a new
// neighbour `n`, the move an enemy will make is `n -> a`, and that is
// the direction `can_step` is asked about.  Get it the other way round
// and the field describes a world where enemies climb what they should
// fall off and fall off what they should climb — invisible while every
// height is 0, which is every world dryopea had before F6.
//
// The visible consequence is that a `wall` beside the core JOINS a
// robot's field: a robot cannot climb onto it, but one that somehow
// stood there could step down and walk home, and the field says so.
// Nothing routes a robot ONTO it, because `flow_steps` checks the step
// as well as the distance.
//
// ── ⚠ A struct cannot ride home on two tail calls (loft#880) ────
//
// `flow_build` and `flow_desire` bind the sweep's result to a local
// and return the local, which looks like noise and is not.  A struct
// returned through TWO nested tail-position calls arrives EMPTY: with
// `flow_build` written as a bare `flow_sweep(...)`, a caller's own
// one-line wrapper (`fn field_for(k, pw) { flow_build(...) }`) got a
// field with zero cells.
//
// It is the same family as loft#877 — which dryopea filed at F5c for
// an INDEX on a call's result in tail position — and it bites the same
// way: silently, with a value that is structurally valid and
// completely empty.  Here it emptied every flow field on the map and
// stopped the entire game, and the 9 red tests all named the movement
// they no longer did rather than the wrapper that lost the data.
//
// ── The field is DERIVED, so it is recomputed and never saved ────
//
// [`plans/07`](../plans/07-shared-world-substrate/README.md)
// § Evaluated: authored → a layer, derived → recompute, runtime →
// sim state.  This is the middle one.  Plan 11's open question 2
// asked whether `hex_field::Labels` should host it instead; the
// answer measured at F2 is no, not today — `Labels` is a BOUNDED
// rectangle (`labels_new(q0, r0, w, h)`) addressed in **odd-r**,
// while dryopea's world is unbounded, sparse and axial until plan 09.
// Nothing in the `hex_*` family carries a distance field to reuse
// either; the closest is `hex_shape::flood_outside`, which is
// boolean reachability over a bounded box.

use lattice;
use palette;
use painted;
use height;
use passable;

// No route to the core.
//
// Larger than any distance a real map can produce, so that "smaller
// is better" comparisons refuse it without knowing it exists — see
// § Three states above.  Small enough that adding a step to it cannot
// overflow an i64 anywhere.
pub const FLOW_UNREACHABLE: integer = 1000000000;

// A cell's build is abandoned past this many reachable hexes, and
// `truncated` says so.
//
// The BFS terminates on its own today, because every passable hex is
// a painted one (an unpainted hex is sea, and sea is not walkable) and
// the painted set is finite.  That argument rests on the PALETTE
// though — make sea walkable and the frontier expands forever over an
// unbounded plane.  A cap turns that from a hang into a fault with a
// name, and `truncated` is asserted false by the tests so it cannot
// quietly become the normal case.
pub const FLOW_MAX_CELLS: integer = 1048576;

// A climb no step in the game can exceed — the DESIRE field's limit.
//
// Plan 11 phase F7.  `docs/ENEMY_MOVEMENT.md` § Sealing the perimeter
// needs a second sweep in which walls are passable: where an enemy
// still WANTS to go when no route exists.  "Walls are passable" is
// exactly "the climb does not bind", so the desire field is this
// number and nothing else — see `flow_desire`.
pub const FLOW_CLIMB_ANY: float = 1000000.0;

// One cell's distance and the height the world had there when the
// field was built.
//
// The height is stored rather than re-read (plan 11 F6) so that
// `flow_steps` can check the STEP without the world as a parameter.
// It is not an optimisation: the alternative is four more arguments on
// the function the mover calls per enemy per tick, and a field that
// could silently be asked about a world it was not built for.  Kept
// here, a field is self-describing — the same argument that put the
// core on `FlowField` at F2.
pub struct FlowCell {
    q:      integer,
    r:      integer,
    dist:   integer,
    height: float,
}

// A finished field: the reachable cells, the core they are distances
// TO, and the climb limit they were swept with.
//
// Only cells with a route are stored — a field over a sparse world is
// itself sparse, and every miss is `FLOW_UNREACHABLE`.  The core is
// kept because a field without its source is ambiguous, and F5b's
// approach→engage handoff needs to know which core it is steering to.
//
// `climb` is the class's WHOLE contribution to this field (plan 11
// F6 — `passable.loft` § climb_limit), which is why `spawn.loft`
// builds one field per climb limit rather than one per class, and why
// `flow_steps` needs no `kind`.
pub struct FlowField {
    cells:     hash<FlowCell[q, r]>,
    core:      Hex,
    climb:     float,
    truncated: boolean,
}

// How many steps from (q, r) to the core, or `FLOW_UNREACHABLE`.
//
// ⚠ **The only door.**  Reading `f.cells[q, r].dist` directly gives
// null for a cell with no route, and `?` turns that into 0 — "at the
// core". Ask here instead.
pub fn flow_distance(f: const FlowField, q: integer, r: integer) -> integer {
    fd_c = f.cells[q, r];
    if fd_c == null { return FLOW_UNREACHABLE; }
    fd_c.dist
}

// Is there any route from (q, r) to the core?
pub fn flow_reachable(f: const FlowField, q: integer, r: integer) -> boolean {
    flow_distance(f, q, r) < FLOW_UNREACHABLE
}

// How many hexes can reach the core at all — the size of the field.
pub fn flow_count(f: const FlowField) -> integer {
    len(f.cells)
}

// The height the world had at (q, r) when this field was swept, in
// metres — what `flow_steps` measures its steps against.
//
// `0.0` for a cell the field does not hold, which is meaningless
// rather than wrong: a cell with no route has no step to measure.  Ask
// `flow_reachable` first, exactly as with `flow_distance`.
pub fn flow_height(f: const FlowField, q: integer, r: integer) -> float {
    fh_c = f.cells[q, r];
    if fh_c == null { return 0.0; }
    fh_c.height
}

// Build the field for one enemy class: a breadth-first sweep out from
// the core over the steps that class can take.
//
// One field per class, not one field with per-class edge weights —
// plan 11 F0 settled that: the palette's movement data is boolean, so
// there is nothing to weigh an edge with.  `kind` reaches the sweep
// ONLY through `climb_limit`, which is what lets `spawn.loft` share
// one field between classes that climb the same (plan 11 F6).
//
// A core the class cannot stand on yields an EMPTY field, where every
// hex — including the core's own — is unreachable.  That is the
// honest answer rather than a special case: nothing can walk to a
// place nothing can stand.
pub fn flow_build(pal: vector<GroundType>, pw: PaintedWorld, hl: const HeightLayer,
                  kind: u8, core: Hex) -> FlowField {
    // ⚠ Bound, not returned as a bare tail call — see § A struct
    // cannot ride home on two tail calls, below.
    fb_out = flow_sweep(pal, pw, hl, climb_limit(kind), core);
    fb_out
}

// Where an enemy WANTS to go when it has no route at all — the same
// sweep with the climb lifted, so walls are passable.
//
// Plan 11 phase F7, and `docs/ENEMY_MOVEMENT.md` § Sealing the
// perimeter is what it is for: *"Blocked enemies still want the core,
// and they arrive already spread… each enemy attacks the wall where
// ITS OWN route to the core first meets it."*  Following this gradient
// until the height rule refuses a step is what makes the target
// per-enemy; "the nearest wall", computed once, hands every enemy the
// same hex and collapses the siege to one chokepoint.
//
// ⚠ **One field for every class**, unlike `flow_build`.  The only
// thing a class contributes to passability is its climb
// (`passable.loft` § climb_limit), and this sweep drops the climb —
// so there is nothing left for a class to change.  Give a class its
// own SURFACE rule and that stops being true, which is the case
// `hex_walkable`'s note is watching for.
pub fn flow_desire(pal: vector<GroundType>, pw: PaintedWorld, hl: const HeightLayer,
                   core: Hex) -> FlowField {
    fd_out = flow_sweep(pal, pw, hl, FLOW_CLIMB_ANY, core);
    fd_out
}

// An empty field — no cells, so every hex is unreachable and nothing
// is ever offered as a step.
//
// What a caller passes for "there is no such field here": a mover
// handed one moves nobody, which is a visible failure rather than a
// wrong move.
pub fn flow_empty() -> FlowField {
    FlowField { cells: [], core: Hex { q: 0, r: 0 }, climb: 0.0, truncated: false }
}

// The sweep both fields are.  `climb` is the only thing that differs
// between routing and desire.
fn flow_sweep(pal: vector<GroundType>, pw: PaintedWorld, hl: const HeightLayer,
              climb: float, core: Hex) -> FlowField {
    fb_f = FlowField { cells: [], core: core, climb: climb, truncated: false };
    if !hex_walkable(pal, pw, hl, core.q, core.r) { return fb_f; }

    fb_f.cells[core.q, core.r] = FlowCell {
        q: core.q, r: core.r, dist: 0,
        height: hex_height(pal, pw, hl, core.q, core.r),
    };
    fb_frontier: vector<Hex> = [core];
    fb_d = 0;

    // Frontier by frontier rather than cell by cell: every hex in one
    // frontier is the same distance out, so a cell's distance is the
    // ring it was found in and needs no per-cell bookkeeping.
    while len(fb_frontier) > 0 {
        fb_d += 1;
        fb_next: vector<Hex> = [];
        for fb_h in fb_frontier {
            for fb_dir in 0..6 {
                fb_n = lat_neighbour(fb_h, fb_dir);
                // Already labelled — and its label is smaller or
                // equal, because BFS reaches every cell by a shortest
                // route first.
                if fb_f.cells[fb_n.q, fb_n.r] != null { continue; }
                // ⚠ `fb_n -> fb_h`, not the other way round: the sweep
                // runs outward and the ENEMY walks inward, so the move
                // being tested is the one it will actually make.  See
                // § What the field is over.
                if !can_climb(pal, pw, hl, climb, fb_n, fb_h) { continue; }
                fb_f.cells[fb_n.q, fb_n.r] = FlowCell {
                    q: fb_n.q, r: fb_n.r, dist: fb_d,
                    height: hex_height(pal, pw, hl, fb_n.q, fb_n.r),
                };
                fb_next += [fb_n];
            }
        }
        if len(fb_f.cells) > FLOW_MAX_CELLS {
            fb_f.truncated = true;
            return fb_f;
        }
        fb_frontier = fb_next;
    }
    fb_f
}

// ─── The arrow ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Plan 11 phase F3.  Which neighbour of (q, r) is closest to the
// core — the step an enemy standing there wants to take.
//
// ── It is COMPUTED, never stored ────────────────────────────────
//
// The field holds distances and this reads them.  Baking one
// direction per cell into `FlowCell` would be cheaper per query and
// would make F5c impossible: enemies do not queue, so one whose step
// is taken by a companion moves *beside* them, which needs the whole
// preference ordering over the six neighbours at move time and not a
// single precomputed answer (plan 11 § The field must store
// DISTANCE).  Discovering that after the representation was chosen
// would mean rebuilding it.
//
// So F5's mover will read distances too, and this function is the
// field's INVARIANT rather than the mover's implementation: follow
// the arrows from any reachable cell and the core is exactly
// `distance` steps away.  `tests/11_f3_flow_arrows.loft` sweeps every
// cell of several worlds to check it.
//
// ── Ties break by direction order, and that is load-bearing ─────
//
// Two neighbours are often equally close, and the lowest direction
// index wins.  Any deterministic rule would do; having NO rule would
// not — plan 08 gates the game by replaying written-down runs, and a
// tie broken differently on a different day makes every asserted
// number unrepeatable.
//
// ── Why a stall is the only way this can go wrong ───────────────
//
// A step is taken only to a *strictly* smaller distance, so the walk
// cannot cycle: a cycle would need a distance to return to a value it
// has already left. Termination is free for the same reason. What is
// left is one failure mode — a cell with no smaller neighbour, i.e. a
// local minimum, where an enemy would stand forever. That is what the
// sweep is looking for.
//
// Returns the hex ITSELF when there is no better neighbour, which
// covers both honest cases — at the core, or nowhere to go — so a
// caller that ignores the answer moves nobody rather than moving
// somebody somewhere wrong. Ask `flow_distance` / `flow_reachable` to
// tell "arrived" from "stuck".
pub fn flow_step(f: const FlowField, q: integer, r: integer) -> Hex {
    fs_all = flow_steps(f, q, r);
    fs_all[0] ?? Hex { q: q, r: r }
}

// Every neighbour of (q, r) that is closer to the core, BEST FIRST —
// the whole preference ordering `flow_step` returns the head of.
//
// Plan 11 phase F5c.  Enemies do not queue: one whose step is taken by
// a companion moves BESIDE them (`docs/ENEMY_MOVEMENT.md` § Sealing
// the perimeter), so the mover needs every acceptable step in
// preference order and not just the winner.  That is the requirement
// F3 chose the representation for — a baked direction per cell could
// only ever have answered the head of this list.
//
// ⚠ **Closer is not enough — the STEP has to be legal too** (plan 11
// F6).  Since the sweep tests edges in the enemy's direction of
// travel, a cell can be in the field and still be unreachable FROM
// here: a `wall` beside the core carries a small distance for a robot
// (it could step down and walk home) and a robot on the ground beside
// it must not be told to climb up there.  So an offered neighbour is
// one that is both closer and within `f.climb` of this cell's height.
//
// ⚠ **In a BFS field every entry here still carries the SAME
// distance**, `d - 1`.  If the step `here -> n` is legal then the
// sweep tried exactly that edge when it expanded `n`, so `here` was
// labelled at no more than `dist(n) + 1`; combined with "strictly
// closer" that leaves one value.  The ordering is therefore direction
// order alone — F3's tie-break — and a caller that skips an occupied
// entry loses nothing but that hex.
//
// Empty at the core (nothing is closer than 0) and empty on a cell
// with no route, so a caller that moves to `[0]` and otherwise stays
// put handles both without knowing they are different — ask
// `flow_distance` / `flow_reachable` to tell "arrived" from "stuck".
pub fn flow_steps(f: const FlowField, q: integer, r: integer) -> vector<Hex> {
    fss_out: vector<Hex> = [];
    fss_d = flow_distance(f, q, r);
    if fss_d >= FLOW_UNREACHABLE { return fss_out; }
    fss_h = flow_height(f, q, r);
    for fss_n in lat_neighbours(Hex { q: q, r: r }) {
        // Strictly smaller, so an equal neighbour is never a step —
        // this is what makes the walk terminate, and the direction
        // order it is collected in is the tie-break above.
        if flow_distance(f, fss_n.q, fss_n.r) < fss_d
            && flow_height(f, fss_n.q, fss_n.r) - fss_h <= f.climb {
            fss_out += [fss_n];
        }
    }
    fss_out
}
